    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n10402" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀντίπους</span></b> , ὁ, ἡ, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">πουν</span>, τό, gen. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οδος</span>,
			<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>with the feet opposite,</i>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">στὰς ἀ.,</span> of one <i>at the
				Antipodes,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>Ti.</u>
				63a
			</span></i>; so 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀ. ἔσται πορευόμενος ἕκαστος αὐτὸς
					αὑτῷ</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Arist.
					<u>Cael.</u>
					308b20
				</span></i>
			, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Eratosth.
				16.19
			</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οἱ ἀ.</span>”
				<i>the Antipodes,</i>
				 <i><span class="en">
					Str.
					1.1.13
				</span></i>
			, <i><span class="en">
				Cleom.
				1.2
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Cic.
				<u>Acad.Pr.</u>
				2.39.123
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Plu.
				2.869c
			</span></i>.</div>
